WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.760
Here's a question for aspiring screenwriters:

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:10.000
Is it possible to make the movie "Passengers" better with some relatively minor changes?

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.520
You know, I don't think this is a really bad film.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.420
After hearing reviews from friends and online, I sort of expected it to be.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:21.900
But I think that feeling was mostly disappointed expectation. Passengers is by no means a train wreck.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:26.280
It's just really, really predictable. The plot goes like this:

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:32.240
After the interstellar spaceship, Avalon, malfunctions, Chris Pratt wakes up from hyper sleep ninety years before he's supposed to.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.800
Ninety years before anyone else on the ship.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.520
He can't go back to sleep. He can't contact earth. He can't get to the ship's controls.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.320
He tries to do all of these things and fails.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.960
As a year passes, he grows increasingly sad and hopeless.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:51.180
So eventually he decides to wake up another passenger, Jennifer Lawrence, condemning her to the same fate, essentially death.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:55.140
In order to give himself some more company. He acts like it's another malfunction.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.100
He doesn't tell her. After a while, they fall in love.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:03.600
She finds out the truth, gets really mad, and then the ship begins to really malfunction.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.700
So Pratt sacrifices himself to save it, but in the end, she saves him.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:12.740
They fall in love again, and spend the rest of their lives alone on the ship, happily ever after.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.940
Oh and Laurence Fishburne's in it, too.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:18.580
"How long have I got?"
About twelve or thirteen minutes in, Act III.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:22.840
But, you know, count your blessings, it's way better than what oscar-nominated actor Andy Garcia got.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.560
*audience disappointment* 
So you can break this plot up into five basic turning points:

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.520
1. Chris wakes up.
2. Chris wakes Jennifer up.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.620
3. She finds out.
4. Chris sacrifices himself.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:39.000
5. She forgives him. 
The most acute tension in the film happens between parts 2 and 3.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.300
When Jennifer starts to fall in love with Chris, but doesn't yet know the truth.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.200
We know the truth is coming; there's a little dramatic irony there.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:54.360
And so we wait with increasing anxiety until it does. And when it does, predictably, Jennifer freaks out, predictably, and we feel a little sadness. Predictably.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:59.640
At this point, exactly an hour into the movie, the script has sort of written itself into a corner.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:06.340
Because Pratt is the real main character, because we spend 30 minutes alone with him at the start of the film and see everything through his eyes,

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:11.100
There's really only two paths to take regarding the central relationship.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:17.040
Either he dies, or she forgives him. In both ways he finds absolution for the sin he committed.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:21.220
Because you're not going to be able to turn him bad, not a character like this.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.200
Not unless you have five seasons to do it.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:29.660
And yes, I know there are some allusions to "The Shining" here, but "The Shining" spent pretty much no time at all endearing us

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:35.920
to the character of Jack Torrance. Passengers spends the whole first act endearing us to the lovable, scrappy Chris Pratt.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:42.040
It's clear the creators, or the studio, were intent on this being a love story, so they chose to go with the schmaltzy ending.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:49.260
But they could have chosen either. The last 45 minutes would have been the same; a movie pretty much devoid of real tension or suspense.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:56.060
Which requires a completely new plot device in the third act, the total malfunction of the ship, to juice the ending with some adrenaline.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:02.860
You know a couple weeks ago, I was watching Chris Stuckmann and Doug Walker's joint review of "Passengers," and Walker said something interesting.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:08.840
"If they told this in a different order,
it would have been ten times more interesting."

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:17.560
The thing I began to wonder is what if you rearranged the movie. So that Jennifer Lawrence was the point-of-view character, not Chris Pratt?

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:22.140
What if the film began with her experience? Well I couldn't stop thinking about it,

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:27.120
So I re-edited the existing footage-see what would happen to the story

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:46.420
"Good morning, Aurora. How are you feeling? You just spent 120 years in suspended animation-" *malfunction*

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:52.920
"Hello?"

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:57.980
"Anybody?"

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:05.780
"Hello."
"Hi, are you a passenger or crew?"

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:15.400
"Passenger. Jim Preston." 
"Do you know what's going on? Nobody else from my row woke up."

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:22.580
"Same for me." 
When I made this change and just watched from there, some interesting things started to happen

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:29.160
First of all, the first act changes its tone entirely. With the original first act, you're wandering around the ship with Chris Pratt.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:35.760
You learn cool things. You see him come to terms of the situation, have fun, get depressed, and finally reach the

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:40.660
ethical dilemma that's meant to animate the movie. What you see is what you get here.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.960
Which makes for somewhat passive viewing experience.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:49.460
It's an amusing sequence, but that's mostly down to cool production design and Pratt's always charming performance.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.240
"Guess I am going to die of old age on this ship."

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:57.440
But in the new first act, the story is infused from the start with mystery.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:05.320
When I made the editing changes, I was genuinely surprised by just how creepy Pratt's character is when you take away his point of view.

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:12.080
We don't know whether to trust him or not, believe him or not. And that makes the audience's experience an active one in every moment.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:15.920
"Do you know what's going on? Nobody else from my row woke up."

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:24.640
"Same for me." As he leads Jennifer around the ship, locations become crime scenes where the viewer tries to figure out what's happening.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:27.980
This, of course by the way, is what makes Andrei Tarkovsky's "Solaris" so great.

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:36.240
So much has happened on Solaris by the time the lead character arrives that he and the audience have to piece the fragments together themselves.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:41.699
I mean, just look at how creepy he is. I actually think that Pratt's performance takes on more dimensions from this angle.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:46.620
You're not always forced to see him as sympathetic, so his darker side comes out in greater relief.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:54.340
More important than that, the dark choice that sits at the heart of this film hits with greater impact when seen for the first time from Lawrence's perspective

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:57.140
because we feel the cruelty as she feels it.

00:05:57.340 --> 00:05:58.880
"Did you wake me up?"

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:02.100
"I tried not to."

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:02.880
*gasp*

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:07.460
Now we're at the point where the original story hits that dead end.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.880
But with this new edit, you have some more options to play with.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:16.280
Namely, with this configuration, you could actually turn Pratt into the villain, the original film suggests,

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:22.900
But can't follow through on and make "Passengers" into the thriller horror film that so many critics wanted to see.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:31.520
You can even cut back in that whole first section of Chris's time alone on the ship, but put it after the revelation without the need to justify his good guy-ness.

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:35.200
You could show a deeper, more desperate side to his loneliness.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:41.220
Something that I think actually would make him more sympathetic in the end and the choice he makes more agonizing.

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:56.460
*gasp*

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:05.420
Of course, this simple cut and paste doesn't solve every problem in the script. The ending, for example, is still an issue.

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:12.380
I think you'd want to remove the malfunction McGuffin, but it might be just as trite to close the story in true horror fashion.

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:18.280
It could be cool to give it a twilight zone ending as Peter's last film suggested and have Pratt die and Lawrence gets

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:21.920
so lonely that she begins to contemplate the same horrible choice.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:28.420
Brainstorming like this is obviously really fun and much easier than actually writing a movie and making one.

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:35.860
For all its faults, "Passengers is a big-budget movie with a completely original script, and that should be celebrated because it's a dying breed.

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:40.560
But a film like this is a good opportunity for would-be screenwriters to think about story.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:45.940
Because you can learn just as much from films that don't work as you can from those that do.

